WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.400
Last, the waves themselves.

2
00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:04.725
Three things make them grow.

3
00:00:04.975 --> 00:00:11.125
Wind speed, fetch, and duration: how hard it blows, over how much water, and for how long.

4
00:00:11.375 --> 00:00:15.575
Fetch is the distance of open water the wind blows across.

5
00:00:15.825 --> 00:00:18.725
So picture a twenty-knot wind off the land.

6
00:00:18.975 --> 00:00:21.625
Near the beach, is it rough or smooth?

7
00:00:22.625 --> 00:00:24.100
Smooth.

8
00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:26.050
There’s no fetch.

9
00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:32.250
Turn that same wind onshore, across a hundred miles of Gulf, and it builds real seas.

10
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:36.100
Swell is waves that have left the place where they were made.

11
00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:41.050
It can arrive well ahead of a distant storm, on a perfectly calm day.

12
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:47.300
Forecast wave heights are significant wave heights: the average of the highest third of the waves.

13
00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:51.675
So expect some waves much larger, now and then about double.

14
00:00:51.925 --> 00:00:55.825
A four-foot forecast includes the occasional eight-footer.

15
00:00:56.075 --> 00:00:58.650
That completes Navigation General.

16
00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:02.800
Next comes chart plotting, where we put all of this on paper.
